# scratch-boring — Boring-authored core: value system, block AST, evaluator,
# linker, scheduler, and (this file) the Bevy ECS wiring around all of it.
#
# Split across several sibling files in this same directory (all pulled in
# below via a bare `use <name>`, the same same-project multi-file mechanism
# `sb3_loader.br` was the first to use here -- see its own header comment
# for the full explanation: every top-level item in a bare-`use`d sibling
# file is spliced into the same generated Rust file this file's own code
# lives in, sharing the `use bevy.prelude.*`/`use numlib.big_num.*` below
# project-wide, so none of them redeclare either). This file used to hold
# everything directly; it was split once it grew past ~3100 lines:
#
# - `sb3_loader.br` — raw `.sb3`/`project.json` -> plain structs (`Sb3Project` etc.)
# - `values.br` — `ScratchNumber`/`ScratchValue`, the value model
# - `runtime.br` — `Block`/`BlockKind`, `StopSignal`, and every runtime resource shape
# - `linker.br` — `Sb3Project` -> real runtime resources + `Block` AST
# - `interpreter.br` — `eval`/`exec`, the tree-walking evaluator itself
# - `scheduler.br` — Phase 1's run-to-completion scheduler + test entry points
# - `assets.br` — costume/sound extraction from a `.sb3` zip
# - `scratch.br` (this file) — Phase 2's Bevy ECS wiring: components, systems,
#   `build_app`/`run_game`/`main`
#
# `BigUint`/`BigInt`/`BigFraction`/`ScratchNumber` (exact arithmetic, in
# `values.br`) are 100% Boring-authored. `BigUint`/`BigInt`/`BigFraction`
# themselves live in the sibling ../boring-numlib project (imported below
# via this project's boring.toml [deps]) -- extracted out of this project
# into boring-numlib so other Boring projects can depend on the same types.
# `ScratchNumber`/`ScratchNumberKind` (built on top of them) stay in
# `values.br`, since they're specific to this project. This project is
# 100% Boring-authored, same as breakout-boring -- no hand-written Rust
# module remains.
use sb3_loader
use values
use runtime
use linker
use interpreter
use scheduler
use assets
use pen

use bevy.prelude.*
# `Volume` (for `sound_setvolumeto`/`changevolumeby`'s own `PlaybackSettings.
# with_volume` call, below) isn't in `bevy_audio`'s own prelude module
# (only `GlobalVolume` is -- confirmed by reading that crate's own
# `src/lib.rs`), so `bevy.prelude.*` alone doesn't bring it in; it needs
# its own explicit import from the crate root re-export.
use bevy.audio.Volume
# `KeyboardInput`/`ButtonState` (for `handle_ask_text_input`'s own real
# character-by-character text capture, below) aren't re-exported at the
# `bevy::input` path at all (confirmed: `bevy_input`'s own `src/lib.rs`
# prelude module doesn't include them, and `bevy::input` only re-exports
# that same prelude -- a real E0603 "private struct" confirmed with
# `cargo build`) -- needs a direct `bevy_input` dependency (`Cargo.toml`)
# to name `bevy_input::keyboard::...` at all, same reasoning `Volume`'s
# own doc comment above already established for a different crate.
# `MessageReader<T>` (not `EventReader<T>` -- confirmed against real
# `bevy_ecs` source: Bevy 0.19 renamed events to "messages" project-wide,
# `EventReader`/`Events` -> `MessageReader`/`Messages`, `KeyboardInput`
# itself derives `Message` now, not `Event`) is already in `bevy_ecs`'s
# own prelude, reachable through the existing `bevy.prelude.*` below --
# no separate import needed for that one.
use bevy_input.keyboard.KeyboardInput
# `ButtonState` itself lives one level up from `KeyboardInput` -- confirmed
# with a real `cargo build` E0603 that `bevy_input::keyboard::ButtonState`
# is a private re-export; the real, public definition is `bevy_input::
# ButtonState` directly (shared across keyboard/mouse/gamepad, not
# keyboard-specific).
use bevy_input.ButtonState
# `Anchor` isn't re-exported through `bevy_sprite::prelude` (confirmed
# against real `bevy_sprite` source -- that module only re-exports
# `Sprite`/`SpriteImageMode`/`SpriteMesh`/texture-slice types/`Text2d*`),
# so `bevy.prelude.*` doesn't surface it either -- same "one level up from
# where you'd expect" shape as `ButtonState` just above.
use bevy_sprite.Anchor

# BigUint/BigInt/BigFraction/BigUintError/BigFractionError: pure-Boring
# exact-arithmetic tower, imported from ../boring-numlib via this project's
# boring.toml [deps] (numlib = "../boring-numlib") rather than embedded here
# -- see that project's README for why it was extracted into its own repo
# (shared with other Boring projects, e.g. whisper-boring, instead of
# copy-pasted). This is the real, load-bearing implementation backing
# `ScratchValue.Num` (`values.br`): it replaces the hand-written Rust
# `src/value_rt.rs` (which used `num-bigint`/`num-rational`) entirely.
use numlib.big_num.*

# ─── Phase 2: Bevy ECS wiring ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This is the "real per-frame scheduler" half of the user's chosen Phase 2
# scope (rendering + scheduler together, not scheduler-only or rendering-
# only). Script execution now happens inside a Bevy `Update` system, driven
# by the engine's own tick, instead of a plain synchronous call from a test
# or `main`. Honest framing, not a shortcut: no currently-supported block can
# actually span more than one frame yet (no `wait`/`forever` -- see
# README.md's roadmap), so every queued script still runs to completion
# within the single `Update` tick it's picked up in. What changes here is
# *who owns when a script runs* (Bevy's tick, not the caller) -- genuine
# mid-script pausing is future work for when `wait`/`forever` land, at which
# point `run_pending_threads` below is exactly the system that needs to grow
# a real per-tick work budget instead of draining each thread to completion.
#
# Vertical-slice scope for this first pass: load exactly one project (path
# given at `build_app` time via the `ProjectPath` resource), queue and run
# its green-flag script(s) once at Startup via `PendingThreads`, and render
# each target as a solid-color placeholder rect at its saved position --
# real costume bitmap/SVG rendering is untested territory here (only ever
# proven in breakout-boring's own hand-authored, non-costume sprites),
# deliberately deferred to a later Phase 2 pass per README.md rather than
# faked with a wrong shortcut.
#
# `Vars`/`Lists`/`ProcTable`/`Broadcasts` are already `as Resource` (see
# their own doc comments above) -- that refactor was purely additive, the
# whole Phase 1 test suite still passes unmodified calling them as plain
# values the way it always did.

# Marker component for the one entity spawned per Scratch target (Stage +
# sprites). `target_id` (added for Motion) matches a `Positions` table key
# (see that struct's own doc comment on why it's the target's *name*) --
# `sync_transforms_from_positions` below uses it to find which `Position`
# entry belongs to which entity's `Transform`.
pub struct ScratchSprite as Component, Debug:
    string target_id

# Every costume this sprite's own target has, pre-loaded as a real Bevy
# `Handle<Image>` at Startup (see `setup_scratch_project`'s own spawn
# loop) -- what makes `looks_switchcostumeto`/`looks_nextcostume`
# (`LooksState.costume_index`, mutated at runtime) able to swap a
# sprite's actual rendered image with a plain handle-array index, never
# re-touching the `.sb3` zip after Startup. Attached to *every*
# `ScratchSprite` entity uniformly, even a flat-color-placeholder one
# (raw-JSON test fixtures, or a real costume that failed to extract) --
# `handles = []` there, so `sync_costume_from_looks_states` below can
# stay a single, unconditional system with no per-entity "does this one
# even have costumes" branch to worry about.
pub struct TargetCostumeHandles as Component, Debug, Clone:
    [Handle<Image>] handles

# Rotation style ("all around"/"left-right"/"don't rotate") used to live
# here as its own read-only Component, baked in once at spawn time --
# correct as long as nothing could ever change it after that. `motion_
# setrotationstyle` (a real block, landed later) needs the calling
# script's own instance to change it at runtime, which a plain Component
# can never do (`exec_simple` has no `Commands`/`Query` access at all) --
# moved to `SoundQueue.rotation_styles` instead, a genuine per-instance
# resource `exec_simple` CAN mutate directly, same shape as `volumes`/
# `pitches`. See that field's own doc comment in `runtime.br` for the
# full reasoning and `sync_transforms_from_positions`' own doc comment
# for how rendering reads it now.

# Marker + live lookup key for one sprite's own speech-bubble text entity
# -- a real `ChildOf` child of its `ScratchSprite` entity (spawned right
# alongside it, in `setup_scratch_project`/`spawn_pending_clones`), so its
# `Text2d` visibly sits *above* the sprite instead of sharing its own
# `Transform` (real Scratch's own bubble is a separate on-screen element,
# not text drawn on top of the sprite -- see `sync_speech_bubbles`'s own
# doc comment for the exact vertical-offset formula and why a *child*
# entity, not a same-entity fix, was the right shape for this). `target_id`
# is looked up the same flat way every other sync system already matches
# an entity to its own `Positions`/`LooksStates`/etc entry -- no nested
# parent/child `Query` needed anywhere in this file; `ChildOf` only needs
# to exist for Bevy's own transform-propagation to combine this entity's
# local offset with its parent's world position each frame, nothing this
# project's own code has to do by hand.
pub struct SpeechBubbleAnchor as Component, Debug, Clone:
    string target_id

# Marker + static data for one variable watcher's dedicated entity --
# `variable_id` for the live lookup, `label` (the variable's human name)
# baked in at spawn time since it never changes at runtime (see
# `VariableWatcher`'s own doc comment).
pub struct VariableWatcherTag as Component, Debug:
    string variable_id
    string label

# Marker + static data for one reporter watcher's dedicated entity --
# `target_id`/`expr` for the live `eval()` each tick, `label` baked in at
# spawn time (see `ReporterWatcher`'s own doc comment for why none of this
# ever changes at runtime).
pub struct ReporterWatcherTag as Component, Debug:
    string target_id
    Block expr
    string label

# Marker + static data for one list watcher's dedicated entity --
# `list_id` for the live lookup, `label` baked in at spawn time, same
# reasoning as `VariableWatcherTag`.
pub struct ListWatcherTag as Component, Debug:
    string list_id
    string label

# Marker for `sensing_askandwait`'s own on-screen text-input box -- a
# single always-present entity (spawned once, hidden by default), toggled
# visible by `sync_ask_ui` whenever `Vars.is_asking()`, the same "spawn
# once, toggle `Visibility` every tick" pattern `VariableWatcherTag`'s own
# entities already established (see `data_showvariable`/`hidevariable`'s
# own round). No fields -- unlike the watcher tags above, there's nothing
# static to bake in at spawn time (the question/typed-so-far text both
# change at runtime, read fresh from `Vars` each tick).
pub struct AskBoxText as Component, Debug:
    pass

# Which project to load, set once when `build_app` constructs the `App` --
# read by `setup_scratch_project` below as a `Res<ProjectPath>`. Its own
# resource (rather than a `build_app` parameter closed over directly)
# because Bevy systems only ever receive real system params (Res/ResMut/
# Commands/Query/...), never arbitrary function arguments.
pub struct ProjectPath as Resource, Debug, Clone:
    string path

# Every currently-running `Thread` -- a green-flag click, a keypress hat,
# an `event_whengreaterthan` hat, and a broadcast receiver all start a new
# one the same way (`spawn_thread`), and `run_pending_threads` steps every
# one of them once per `Update` tick (see that system's own doc comment).
# Kept the name `PendingThreads`/`queue` (not renamed to e.g.
# `ActiveThreads`/`threads`) even though a `Thread` can now legitimately
# persist across many ticks rather than always finishing the tick it was
# queued on -- `queue` still accurately describes how a thread *enters*
# this resource (green flag/hats/broadcasts all still queue new ones the
# same way), and keeping the name avoids a mechanical rename across every
# test file that already imports it, for a change that's purely about
# what happens to an entry *after* it's queued, not how queuing itself
# works.
pub struct PendingThreads as Resource, Debug, Clone:
    var mut [Thread] queue

pub def spawn_camera(mut Commands commands):
    commands.spawn(Camera2d)
    # A single, fixed `SpatialListener` for `sound_seteffectto`'s own PAN
    # effect -- see `play_queued_sounds`' own doc comment for the full
    # calibration this pairs with. A tiny ear gap (0.1 world units, NOT
    # Bevy's own default `4.0`) deliberately -- `SpatialListener::default()`'s
    # own gap would make even a *centered* (pan=0) sound quiet, since
    # `rodio`'s own spatial-source formula (confirmed against real source,
    # `rodio::source::spatial::Spatial::set_positions`) applies an inverse-
    # square distance falloff past 1 world unit from either ear, and half
    # of a 4.0 gap (2.0) already exceeds that. This tiny gap, paired with
    # the emitter offset `play_queued_sounds` uses, keeps every emitter
    # within that 1-unit no-falloff radius of both ears at every pan value
    # -- PAN shifts the *balance* between channels without ever silently
    # dropping the overall loudness of a project that uses it.
    commands.spawn((Transform.IDENTITY, SpatialListener.new(0.1)))

# Tries the real `.sb3` (zip) loader first, then falls back to the raw-JSON
# loader -- `setup_scratch_project` (below) needs to accept either shape at
# the same `ProjectPath`, unlike the Phase 1 test helpers which each commit
# to one loader because their caller already knows which fixture kind it's
# passing. A real `.sb3` is what makes `extract_current_costume_to_temp_
# file` (below) able to find any actual costume bytes at all -- a raw-JSON
# fixture has none, so it always falls through to the flat-color placeholder
# regardless of this function's result.
def Sb3Project? load_project_for_boring(string path):
    if let p = Sb3Project.load_for_boring(path.clone()):
        return p
    # NOT a bare tail-expression call to `load_json_for_boring` here -- a
    # `T?`-returning function auto-wraps its tail expression in `Some(...)`
    # without detecting that the expression is *already* an `Option`,
    # double-wrapping it (a real `boring` bug worked around the same way
    # elsewhere in this file, e.g. `block_field_id`/`target_find_hat`). The
    # explicit `if let ... else nil` rewrap avoids that.
    if let p = Sb3Project.load_json_for_boring(path):
        p
    else:
        nil

# Loads the project named by `ProjectPath`, builds every runtime-state
# resource (including `Positions`, for Motion), queues its green-flag
# script(s) to run on the first `Update` tick, and spawns one sprite per
# target at its saved position -- a real costume image if one can be
# extracted (see `extract_current_costume_to_temp_file`'s own doc comment
# for exactly when that succeeds), otherwise the
# original flat-color placeholder rect. All in one Startup system (rather
# than split across several, unlike breakout-boring's many small ones)
# because every piece here is derived from the same loaded `project` value,
# and Bevy Startup systems have no ordinary way to pass a non-`Resource`
# value between each other.
pub def setup_scratch_project(mut Commands commands, Res<ProjectPath> project_path, Res<AssetServer> asset_server):
    if let project = load_project_for_boring(project_path.path.clone()):
        let vars = build_vars(project.clone())
        let lists = build_lists(project.clone())
        let procs = build_procs(project.clone())
        let broadcasts = build_broadcasts(project.clone())
        let positions = build_positions(project.clone())
        let looks_states = build_looks_states(project.clone())
        let target_costumes = build_target_costumes(project.clone())
        let costume_dims = build_target_costume_dims(project_path.path.clone(), project.clone())
        var layer_orders = build_layer_orders(project.clone())
        let key_press_hats = build_key_press_hats(project.clone())
        let click_hats = build_click_hats(project.clone())
        let greater_than_hats = build_greater_than_hats(project.clone())
        let touching_object_hats = build_touching_object_hats(project.clone())
        let backdrop_switch_hats = build_backdrop_switch_hats(project.clone())
        let initial_backdrop = initial_backdrop_name(project.clone())
        let start_as_clone_hats = build_start_as_clone_hats(project.clone())
        let variable_watchers = build_variable_watchers(project.clone())
        let reporter_watchers = build_reporter_watchers(project.clone())
        let list_watchers = build_list_watchers(project.clone())
        let greenflag_scripts = build_greenflag_scripts(project.clone())
        commands.insert_resource(vars)
        commands.insert_resource(lists)
        commands.insert_resource(procs)
        commands.insert_resource(broadcasts)
        commands.insert_resource(positions)
        commands.insert_resource(looks_states)
        commands.insert_resource(target_costumes)
        commands.insert_resource(costume_dims)
        commands.insert_resource(layer_orders)
        commands.insert_resource(KeyPressHats(hats = key_press_hats))
        commands.insert_resource(ClickHats(hats = click_hats))
        commands.insert_resource(GreaterThanHats(hats = greater_than_hats))
        commands.insert_resource(TouchingObjectHats(hats = touching_object_hats))
        commands.insert_resource(BackdropSwitchHats(hats = backdrop_switch_hats.clone(), last_backdrop_name = initial_backdrop))
        commands.insert_resource(start_as_clone_hats)
        # "Nothing pressed, mouse at origin, timer at zero" -- real values
        # arrive on the very next tick via `sync_keyboard_state`/
        # `sync_mouse_state`/`sync_scratch_timer` (all chained before
        # `run_pending_threads` in `build_app`), same as `Positions`' own
        # "starts equal to the target's saved x/y" seeding.
        commands.insert_resource(KeyboardState(pressed_keys = [], any_key_pressed = false))
        commands.insert_resource(MouseState(x = 0.0, y = 0.0, down = false))
        commands.insert_resource(DragState(dragging_id = "", offset_x = 0.0, offset_y = 0.0))
        commands.insert_resource(ScratchTimer(elapsed = 0.0))
        # Same fixed nonzero seed every run -- see `Rng`'s own doc comment
        # for why (a real, documented simplification, not real per-run
        # entropy).
        commands.insert_resource(Rng(state = 88172645463325252 as uint64))
        commands.insert_resource(SoundQueue(play_queue = [], stop_all_requested = false, volumes = build_sound_volume_table(project.clone()), pitches = {=}, pans = {=}, rotation_styles = build_target_rotation_styles(project.clone()), sound_durations = build_target_sound_durations(project.clone()), instance_types = build_instance_types_table(project.clone()), next_clone_id = 0, clone_requests = [], despawn_requests = [], live_clone_count = 0, reset_timer_requested = false, backdrop_switch_hats = backdrop_switch_hats.clone(), draggable = build_target_draggable(project.clone()), pen_states = {=}, pen_line_requests = [], pen_clear_requested = false, stamp_requests = [], scene_composite = blank_scene_composite_data()))
        commands.insert_resource(SpeechBubbles(table = {=}))
        # Every green-flag hat starts its own thread, project-wide -- real
        # Scratch runs them all simultaneously, and now that the real
        # pausable scheduler exists (`PendingThreads` already supports many
        # concurrent threads, same as broadcasts/hats/clone-spawned ones),
        # there's no reason left to run only the first one found (see
        # `build_greenflag_scripts`' own doc comment for the real project
        # this was confirmed to break -- most of `Coconut clicker`'s own
        # setup, score-digit clones, and wave animation never ran under the
        # old "first one only" behavior).
        mut greenflag_threads = []
        for script in greenflag_scripts:
            greenflag_threads.push(spawn_thread(script))
        commands.insert_resource(PendingThreads(queue = greenflag_threads))
        for target in project.targets:
            # Initial placement here still comes straight from the target's
            # own saved x/y (not `Positions`, which is the *live*, mutable
            # copy Motion blocks act on) -- both start out equal,
            # `sync_transforms_from_positions` is what keeps them in sync
            # as scripts run.
            let target_name = "{target.name_string()}"
            let transform = Transform.from_xyz(target.x_or(0.0), target.y_or(0.0), 0.0)
            # `Text2d.new("")` on every target's own entity (not a separate
            # bubble entity) -- see `sync_speech_bubbles`' own doc comment
            # for why this means a bubble currently renders exactly at the
            # sprite's own anchor point rather than visually offset above
            # it (a deliberate simplification, not an oversight).
            if let costume_path = extract_current_costume_to_temp_file(project_path.path.clone(), target_name.clone()):
                # A real costume was extracted (only possible when
                # `ProjectPath` names an actual `.sb3` zip, never a
                # hand-authored raw-JSON fixture -- see that function's own
                # doc comment) -- no `custom_size` override, so it renders
                # at the image's native pixel size. Real Scratch's
                # `bitmapResolution`/per-sprite `size` percent scaling
                # aren't applied yet -- deliberately deferred, see
                # README.md's roadmap.
                #
                # Every *other* costume this target has gets pre-extracted
                # and pre-loaded here too, not just the current one --
                # `TargetCostumeHandles` is what `looks_switchcostumeto`/
                # `looks_nextcostume` actually swap between at runtime (see
                # that struct's own doc comment). A costume that fails to
                # extract on its own falls back to reusing this target's
                # already-known-good current-costume handle, rather than
                # leaving a gap in the array that would shift every later
                # index -- `looks_switchcostumeto`'s own index math assumes
                # `handles.length == costumes.length` for every target.
                mut costume_handles = []
                for i in 0..<target.costumes.length:
                    if let p = extract_costume_at_index_to_temp_file(project_path.path.clone(), target_name.clone(), i):
                        costume_handles.push(asset_server.load(p.toString()))
                    else:
                        costume_handles.push(asset_server.load(costume_path.toString()))
                mut real_costume_root = commands.spawn((
                    ScratchSprite(target_id = target_name.clone()),
                    Sprite.from_image(asset_server.load(costume_path.toString())),
                    TargetCostumeHandles(handles = costume_handles),
                    transform))
                real_costume_root.with_children((parent): drop(parent.spawn((
                    SpeechBubbleAnchor(target_id = target_name),
                    Text2d.new(""),
                    Anchor(Vec2.new(0.0, -1.0)),
                    Transform.from_xyz(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)))))
            else:
                # Fallback placeholder: Stage gets a light backdrop-sized
                # rect, sprites a small colored square -- unchanged from
                # before real costume loading existed. No real image to
                # ever switch between, so `handles = []` (`sync_costume_
                # from_looks_states` no-ops on an empty array, not a
                # missing component -- see `TargetCostumeHandles`' own doc
                # comment for why this stays a plain empty list rather
                # than skipping the component here).
                let color = if target.is_stage: Color.srgb(0.9, 0.9, 0.9) else: Color.srgb(0.2, 0.6, 0.9)
                let size = if target.is_stage: Vec2.new(480.0, 360.0) else: Vec2.new(40.0, 40.0)
                mut placeholder_root = commands.spawn((
                    ScratchSprite(target_id = target_name.clone()),
                    Sprite.from_color(color, size),
                    TargetCostumeHandles(handles = []),
                    transform))
                placeholder_root.with_children((parent): drop(parent.spawn((
                    SpeechBubbleAnchor(target_id = target_name),
                    Text2d.new(""),
                    Anchor(Vec2.new(0.0, -1.0)),
                    Transform.from_xyz(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)))))
        for watcher in variable_watchers:
            # A z of 10.0 (sprites sit at 0.0) so a watcher always renders
            # above every sprite -- real Scratch draws watchers as a
            # permanent stage-level overlay, never occluded by sprites.
            # A real `Visibility` component -- now that `build_variable_
            # watchers` spawns one of these for *every* Stage variable's
            # monitor regardless of its initial state (own doc comment),
            # this needs a starting value here rather than implicitly
            # defaulting to visible; `sync_variable_watchers` keeps it in
            # sync with `Vars.watcher_visible` every tick after this.
            commands.spawn((
                VariableWatcherTag(variable_id = watcher.variable_id, label = watcher.label),
                Text2d.new(""),
                Visibility.Visible,
                Transform.from_xyz(watcher.x, watcher.y, 10.0)))
        for watcher in reporter_watchers:
            # Same "always above every sprite" z=10.0 as a variable
            # watcher's entity, above.
            commands.spawn((
                ReporterWatcherTag(target_id = watcher.target_id, expr = watcher.expr, label = watcher.label),
                Text2d.new(""),
                Transform.from_xyz(watcher.x, watcher.y, 10.0)))
        for watcher in list_watchers:
            # Same z=10.0 as every other watcher entity, above. A real
            # `Visibility` component too -- same "spawns one of these for
            # every list's monitor regardless of its initial state" reason
            # `VariableWatcherTag`'s own spawn just above already needs
            # one for, now that `build_list_watchers` no longer gates on
            # `is_visible()` either; `sync_list_watchers` keeps it in sync
            # with `Lists.watcher_visible` every tick after this.
            commands.spawn((
                ListWatcherTag(list_id = watcher.list_id, label = watcher.label),
                Text2d.new(""),
                Visibility.Visible,
                Transform.from_xyz(watcher.x, watcher.y, 10.0)))
        # `sensing_askandwait`'s own on-screen box -- one entity, always
        # present, hidden until a script actually asks something (`sync_
        # ask_ui` toggles `Visibility` and fills in the real text every
        # tick). Positioned near the bottom of the stage (`y = -160`, real
        # Scratch's own 480x360 stage runs `-180..180`), `z = 10.0` so it
        # renders above sprites, same as every watcher entity above.
        commands.spawn((
            AskBoxText(),
            Text2d.new(""),
            Visibility.Hidden,
            Transform.from_xyz(0.0, -160.0, 10.0)))

# Steps every currently-running `Thread` once -- the real per-frame
# scheduler `step_thread`'s own doc comment describes (`interpreter.br`).
# A thread that finishes this call is simply dropped from the next tick's
# queue; one that's still running (yielded at a loop iteration/`wait`/
# `wait until`) is carried over unchanged; any scripts it started via
# `Broadcast`/`BroadcastAndWait` this step (`thread.spawned`) become
# brand-new threads of their own, added to the same next-tick queue --
# see `Thread`'s own doc comment for why draining `spawned` has to happen
# here, after every thread's own step, rather than a thread reaching into
# `PendingThreads` directly mid-step. `spawned` is reset to `[]` right
# after draining it, every tick, for exactly the same reason `pending.
# queue` itself used to be reset to `[]` right after being drained in the
# old run-to-completion version of this system -- a `Thread` that keeps
# running across many ticks must never re-spawn the same broadcast twice
# just because its own `spawned` list was never cleared.
pub def run_pending_threads(mut ResMut<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<Lists> lists, Res<ProcTable> procs, Res<Broadcasts> broadcasts, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, Res<KeyboardState> keyboard, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<ScratchTimer> timer, mut ResMut<Rng> rng, mut ResMut<LooksStates> looks, mut ResMut<LayerOrders> layer_orders, Res<TargetCostumes> target_costumes, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, mut ResMut<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<SpeechBubbles> speech_bubbles, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    let threads = pending.queue.clone()
    mut still_running = []
    for thread in threads:
        var t = thread
        let done = step_thread(t, vars.pointee, procs.clone(), lists.pointee, broadcasts.clone(), positions.pointee, keyboard.clone(), mouse.clone(), timer.clone(), rng.pointee, looks.pointee, layer_orders.pointee, target_costumes.clone(), costume_dims.clone(), sound_queue.pointee, speech_bubbles.pointee)
        for script in t.spawned.clone():
            still_running.push(spawn_thread(script))
        t.spawned = []
        if !done:
            still_running.push(t)
    pending.queue = still_running

# Marker component for a currently-playing sound entity -- lets
# `sound_stopallsounds` find and despawn every one of them, real Scratch's
# own "stop everything, unconditionally" semantics for that block (not
# scoped to any one target).
pub struct PlayingSound as Component, Debug:
    pass

# Drains `SoundQueue` each tick: handles `stop_all_requested` first (despawn
# every `PlayingSound` entity, matching real Scratch's own ordering --
# stopping is immediate, not deferred behind any queued plays), then spawns
# one `AudioPlayer` entity per queued `sound_play` request. `PlaybackSettings.
# DESPAWN` (not `.ONCE`) -- the entity removes itself once playback finishes,
# so `PlayingSound`'s own query above only ever reflects sounds *actually
# still playing*, not stale finished ones. A request whose sound can't be
# extracted (see `extract_sound_to_temp_file`'s own doc comment -- always
# true for a raw-JSON fixture, no real asset bytes to extract) is silently
# dropped, same "rendering/audio nicety, not correctness-critical" rationale
# as costume loading's own fallback.
pub def play_queued_sounds(mut Commands commands, Res<ProjectPath> project_path, Res<AssetServer> asset_server, mut ResMut<SoundQueue> sound_queue, Query<(Entity, PlayingSound&)> playing_query):
    if sound_queue.stop_all_requested:
        # `Query<(Entity, PlayingSound&)>` (an Entity paired with a real
        # reference-shaped component), not a bare `Query<Entity, ...>` --
        # the bare form transpiles its `for` loop to `.iter().cloned()`,
        # which fails to compile against `Entity` (already `Copy`, not a
        # reference the way `.cloned()` expects) -- confirmed with a real
        # `cargo build` before this was written. The tuple shape (same
        # convention breakout-boring's own `check_for_collisions` already
        # uses for `Query<(Entity, Transform&, ...), ...>`) sidesteps it.
        for entity, marker in playing_query:
            commands.entity(entity).despawn()
        sound_queue.stop_all_requested = false
    let requests = sound_queue.play_queue.clone()
    sound_queue.play_queue = []
    for request in requests:
        let volume = request.volume
        # Real Scratch's own PITCH-to-playback-rate formula (`Scratch3
        # SoundPlayer`'s `_setPitchShift`, confirmed against real scratch-
        # audio/Tone.js source, not guessed): `pitch` is in tenths of a
        # semitone (`SoundQueue.pitches`' own -360..360 range), so semitones
        # = `pitch / 10`, and a frequency ratio of `2^(semitones/12)` --
        # combined, `2^(pitch/120)`. `PlaybackSettings.speed` (confirmed via
        # `bevy_audio`'s own source: "1.0 is normal speed... changes the
        # play speed of the sound") is exactly this same playback-rate
        # multiplier, so real Scratch's own naive (non-pitch-preserving)
        # pitch-shift-via-speed-change maps onto it directly, no
        # approximation needed.
        # `as float32` on the whole result, not relying on `pow`'s own
        # overload resolution to pick the `float32` variant -- confirmed
        # with a real `cargo build` that `pow(2.0 as float32, ...)` still
        # resolves to the `float64` overload here (a real `boring`
        # ambiguity, not something to fight further), leaving a mismatched-
        # types error against `with_speed`'s own `f32` parameter. Casting
        # the final result is simpler and works regardless of which
        # overload actually ran.
        let rate = pow(2.0, request.pitch / 120.0) as float32
        if let sound_path = extract_sound_to_temp_file(project_path.path.clone(), request.target_id, request.sound_name):
            let Handle<AudioSource> handle = asset_server.load(sound_path.toString())
            # `PlaybackSettings.DESPAWN` bound to its own local first, not
            # `PlaybackSettings.DESPAWN.with_volume(...)` chained inline --
            # the transpiler resolves a dotted associated-constant access
            # followed directly by a method call as one long associated-
            # item *path* (`PlaybackSettings::DESPAWN::with_volume`, a real
            # E0223 confirmed with `cargo build`), not "look up the
            # constant's value, then call a method on it". An intermediate
            # binding sidesteps it.
            let base_settings = PlaybackSettings.DESPAWN
            let with_volume = base_settings.with_volume(Volume.Linear(volume / 100.0))
            let settings = with_volume.with_speed(rate)
            # `sound_seteffectto`'s own PAN effect -- `bevy_audio` has no
            # direct stereo-pan primitive of its own, only spatial audio
            # ("implemented via simple left-right stereo panning", its own
            # doc comment), so this repurposes that. Only engaged when
            # `pan != 0.0` -- the overwhelming majority of real projects
            # never touch PAN at all, and spatial mode has a real,
            # documented side effect (below) not worth paying for a
            # project that never asked for it.
            if request.pan == 0.0:
                commands.spawn((PlayingSound(), AudioPlayer(handle), settings))
            else:
                var mut spatial_settings = settings
                spatial_settings.spatial = true
                # Calibrated against `spawn_camera`'s own 0.1-unit
                # `SpatialListener` ear gap and `rodio`'s real spatial-
                # source formula (confirmed against source, `Spatial::
                # set_positions`): `0.9` keeps the offset (up to +/-0.9 at
                # full pan) plus the 0.05-unit ear half-gap under the
                # 1.0-unit no-falloff radius for *both* ears at every pan
                # value, so PAN shifts the balance between channels
                # without dropping the sound's overall loudness. The
                # channel *diff* modifier that formula also applies means
                # this is an honest, moderate stereo bias (roughly 2:1 at
                # full pan), not a hard, silence-the-opposite-channel pan
                # the way a real dedicated `StereoPannerNode` achieves --
                # `bevy_audio` genuinely has nothing closer to offer.
                let offset = (request.pan / 100.0) * 0.9
                commands.spawn((PlayingSound(), AudioPlayer(handle), spatial_settings, Transform.from_xyz(offset, 0.0, 0.0)))

# Drains `SoundQueue.clone_requests`, queued by `control_create_clone_of`'s
# `exec_simple` case (see that case's own comment, `interpreter.br`, and
# `CloneRequest`'s own doc comment, `runtime.br`, for why the actual entity
# spawn is deferred here rather than done inline -- no `Commands`/`Query`
# access inside `exec_simple`, same reason `sound_play` already defers its
# own entity spawn to `play_queued_sounds`). Chained right after `run_
# pending_threads`/`play_queued_sounds`, before every `sync_*_from_*_states`
# system (`build_app`'s own `.chain()`) -- so a clone's freshly-copied
# `Positions`/`LooksStates`/etc. entries (already written synchronously by
# `control_create_clone_of` itself, before this system ever runs) land on
# its brand-new entity's own `Transform`/`Sprite`/costume the very same
# tick it's created, not one tick late.
#
# A nested `Query` match (walking `sprites` once per queued request to find
# the *origin*'s own existing entity) -- an untested pattern in this
# codebase (see `sync_speech_bubbles`'s own doc comment, which deliberately
# avoided needing exactly this), but the only way to clone the origin's own
# `TargetCostumeHandles`/`Sprite` onto the new entity: a clone always
# shares its origin's costume/sound assets, never another clone's (`Target
# Costumes`/`TargetCostumeDims` stay keyed by origin name only, see
# `SoundQueue.instance_types`' own doc comment). `Transform` is cloned too,
# purely as a reasonable starting point -- not load-bearing, since the very
# next systems in the chain overwrite it from the new entity's own already-
# seeded `Positions` entry anyway.
#
# Also starts the new clone's own `when I start as a clone` thread(s), if
# its origin has any (`StartAsCloneHats`, built at link time exactly like
# `Broadcasts`/every other hat-template resource) -- real Scratch's own
# clone-spawn-time hat, distinct from every other hat kind in that it
# fires once per *newly created clone*, not on some external event
# checked every tick. Pushed straight into `PendingThreads.queue` (not
# `Thread.spawned`, which only exists for threads started *from inside*
# another thread's own step) since this is a fresh top-level trigger,
# same as green flag/keypress/click/broadcast.
pub def spawn_pending_clones(mut Commands commands, mut ResMut<SoundQueue> sound_queue, Res<StartAsCloneHats> start_as_clone_hats, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending, Query<(ScratchSprite&, TargetCostumeHandles&, Sprite&, Transform&)> sprites):
    let requests = sound_queue.clone_requests.clone()
    sound_queue.clone_requests = []
    for request in requests:
        for sprite, handles, spr, transform in sprites:
            if sprite.target_id == request.origin_name:
                mut clone_root = commands.spawn((
                    ScratchSprite(target_id = request.new_id.clone()),
                    spr.clone(),
                    handles.clone(),
                    transform.clone()))
                clone_root.with_children((parent): drop(parent.spawn((
                    SpeechBubbleAnchor(target_id = request.new_id.clone()),
                    Text2d.new(""),
                    Anchor(Vec2.new(0.0, -1.0)),
                    Transform.from_xyz(0.0, 0.0, 1.0)))))
                break
        # Start one fresh `Thread` per `when I start as a clone` template
        # this clone's *origin* has (there can be more than one) --
        # `spawn_thread` already builds a correctly fresh `Frame`/`ExecFrame`
        # from a `ReceiverScript`, exactly like every other hat-triggered
        # thread; only the template's own `target_id` needs substituting
        # from the origin's name to this specific new clone's id first.
        # Pushed into `pending.queue` directly (not `spawned`, which only
        # exists for threads spawned *from inside* another thread's own
        # step) -- same as every other hat-check system, since this is a
        # brand-new top-level trigger, not a nested broadcast.
        for template in start_as_clone_hats.get(request.origin_name.clone()):
            let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = request.new_id.clone(), body = template.body)
            pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))

# Drains `SoundQueue.despawn_requests`, queued by `control_delete_this_
# clone`'s `step_thread` arm (`interpreter.br`) -- same deferral reasoning
# as `spawn_pending_clones` above (no `Commands`/`Query` access inside the
# interpreter). Removes the matching `ScratchSprite` entity and every
# per-instance resource entry for that instance id -- without this, a
# project that clones and deletes in a loop would leak one stale entry per
# clone ever created in `Positions`/`LooksStates`/`LayerOrders`/`SoundQueue.
# volumes`/`SpeechBubbles`/`SoundQueue.instance_types` forever. No ordering
# race with a not-yet-stepped new thread is possible: a `CloneRequest`
# queued during tick N only produces an actual `Thread` in `spawn_pending_
# clones` (same tick), which only enters `PendingThreads.queue` -- not
# stepped until tick N+1, the same one-tick lag broadcast-spawned threads
# already have (`run_pending_threads`'s own doc comment) -- so nothing can
# ever reference an instance id before `spawn_pending_clones` has created
# its entity, and nothing can reference it again after this system removes
# it (the calling thread's own stack was already cleared, synchronously, by
# the same `step_thread` call that queued the despawn).
pub def despawn_pending_clones(mut Commands commands, mut ResMut<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, mut ResMut<LooksStates> looks, mut ResMut<LayerOrders> layer_orders, mut ResMut<SpeechBubbles> speech_bubbles, mut ResMut<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<Lists> lists, Query<(Entity, ScratchSprite&)> sprites):
    let requests = sound_queue.despawn_requests.clone()
    sound_queue.despawn_requests = []
    for instance_id in requests:
        for entity, sprite in sprites:
            if sprite.target_id == instance_id:
                commands.entity(entity).despawn()
                break
        positions.remove(instance_id.clone())
        looks.remove(instance_id.clone())
        layer_orders.remove(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_volume(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_pitch(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_pan(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_rotation_style(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_draggable(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_pen_state(instance_id.clone())
        sound_queue.remove_instance_type(instance_id.clone())
        vars.remove_local(instance_id.clone())
        lists.remove_local(instance_id.clone())
        speech_bubbles.remove(instance_id)

# Copies each target's current bubble text (if any) onto its own
# `SpeechBubbleAnchor` entity's `Text2d` -- a real `ChildOf` child of the
# sprite's own entity (see that struct's own doc comment for why), so the
# bubble visibly sits *above* the sprite instead of sharing its own
# `Transform` and rendering right on top of it. `Text2dWriter` (not direct
# field assignment) is the same proven mechanism `breakout-boring`'s own
# `update_scoreboard` already uses for `Text`/`TextUiWriter` --
# `Text2d`/`Text2dWriter` are the 2D-world equivalent of that same
# `TextSection`-based API.
#
# A direct `Query<(SpeechBubbleAnchor&, mut Text2d&, mut Transform&)>`
# field write (`.pointee`, using `Text2d`'s own derived
# `DerefMut<Target=String>`), not `Text2dWriter` -- confirmed with a real
# test run that `Text2dWriter` needs resources only `TextPlugin`'s
# font-layout systems insert (panics with "Resource does not exist"
# without it, the same failure class as an earlier missing
# `AssetServer`/`Window`), while a plain component field write needs
# nothing beyond the component itself existing. `Text2d`'s own
# multi-section/rich-text machinery (what `Text2dWriter` is really for)
# has no use here -- every bubble is always one plain string.
#
# Vertical offset: the anchor's own *local* `Transform.translation.y` (a
# `ChildOf` child's translation is relative to its parent -- Bevy's own
# transform propagation combines it with the sprite's real, live position
# automatically, nothing this system has to track by hand) is set to the
# sprite's own real half-height (`TargetCostumeDims`, scaled by `LooksState.
# size`, exactly the same `self_hh` calculation `Touching`'s own AABB math
# already makes) plus a fixed 10px padding -- not real Scratch's own exact
# scratch-render pixel math (a whole separate WebGL renderer's own
# screen-space bubble-shape/tail/clamping logic, not attempted here), but
# a real, size-aware "sits above the sprite's own top edge" position
# rather than a fixed, sprite-size-oblivious guess. Combined with the
# anchor entity's own `Anchor(Vec2.new(0.0, -1.0))` (set once at spawn
# time, `setup_scratch_project`/`spawn_pending_clones`) -- the text's own
# BOTTOM edge sits at this translation, so it grows upward from there,
# never downward back onto the sprite.
#
# Origin resolution: `TargetCostumeDims` stays keyed by origin name only,
# same as every other lookup into it from a live clone -- resolved via
# `SoundQueue.instance_type_of` first, same established pattern as
# `sync_costume_from_looks_states`'s own identical need.
pub def sync_speech_bubbles(Query<(SpeechBubbleAnchor&, mut Text2d&, mut Transform&)> query, Res<SpeechBubbles> speech_bubbles, Res<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue):
    for anchor, text2d, transform in query:
        let text = speech_bubbles.text_for(anchor.target_id.clone())
        # `.pointee` inserts exactly one `*` (confirmed with a real `cargo
        # build`): `*text2d` is a whole `Text2d`, not the inner `String` --
        # `Text2d`'s own `DerefMut` goes one level further than that, which
        # `.pointee` doesn't chase automatically. Assigning a freshly built
        # `Text2d` (via `.new(...)`, the generic-associated-function path,
        # not the `Text2d(...)` tuple-literal form -- that hits the same
        # `[external_types]`-whitelist concern `AudioPlayer` needed) is the
        # correct shape at this exact level, not a workaround.
        text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("{text}".to_string())

        let s = looks.get(anchor.target_id.clone())
        let origin = sound_queue.instance_type_of(anchor.target_id.clone())
        let idx = s.costume_index
        let dim = costume_dims.get(origin, idx)
        let scale = s.size / 100.0
        let hh = (dim.height * scale) / 2.0
        transform.translation.y = hh + 10.0

# Same direct-field-write shape as `sync_speech_bubbles` (not
# `Text2dWriter`, same reasoning) -- updates every variable watcher
# entity's `Text2d` to `"<label>: <current value>"` each tick. Reads
# `Vars` directly rather than needing a `label` lookup elsewhere: the
# watcher's `label` was already baked into its own `VariableWatcherTag` at
# spawn time (see `setup_scratch_project`), so this is a single query, no
# separate name resolution needed at read time.
pub def sync_variable_watchers(Query<(VariableWatcherTag&, mut Text2d&, mut Visibility&)> query, Res<Vars> vars):
    for tag, text2d, visibility in query:
        # A sprite-local variable's watcher only ever shows the *origin*
        # sprite's own current value -- clones never get their own
        # separate watcher UI (watchers are built once from `project.
        # monitors`, never touched by clone creation, matching real
        # Scratch). `Vars.owner_of` resolves that live: `""` for a global
        # (harmless as a `calling_instance_id` there, `get`'s own global
        # branch ignores it), else the owning sprite's own name -- which
        # is also its own instance id (identity-seeded, same convention
        # `SoundQueue.instance_types` established).
        let value = vars.get(tag.variable_id.clone(), vars.owner_of(tag.variable_id.clone()))
        text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("{tag.label}: {value}".to_string())
        # `data_showvariable`/`data_hidevariable`'s own real effect --
        # same "write the real component every tick" pattern `sync_
        # visibility_and_size_from_looks_states` already uses for sprites.
        visibility.pointee = if vars.watcher_shown(tag.variable_id.clone()): Visibility.Visible else: Visibility.Hidden

# Same shape as `sync_variable_watchers`, but re-`eval()`s the watcher's
# baked-in `Block` every tick instead of a plain `Vars.get` lookup -- a
# reporter watcher's value (e.g. a sprite's x position) isn't sitting in
# any resource on its own, it's computed the exact same way any other
# script's `XPosition`/`YPosition`/`Direction` reporter is. Needs the same
# full `eval` context `check_greater_than_hats` threads through (every
# resource `eval` can possibly read, since `Block` is generic and this
# system doesn't know ahead of time which of the three it holds) even
# though these three reporters only ever actually touch `positions`.
pub def sync_reporter_watchers(Query<(ReporterWatcherTag&, mut Text2d&)> query, mut ResMut<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<Lists> lists, Res<ProcTable> procs, Res<Broadcasts> broadcasts, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, Res<KeyboardState> keyboard, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<ScratchTimer> timer, mut ResMut<Rng> rng, mut ResMut<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumes> target_costumes, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue):
    for tag, text2d in query:
        let top_frame = Frame(params = {=}, target_id = tag.target_id.clone())
        let value = eval(tag.expr.clone(), vars.pointee, top_frame, procs.clone(), lists.pointee, broadcasts.clone(), positions.pointee, keyboard.clone(), mouse.clone(), timer.clone(), rng.pointee, looks.pointee, target_costumes.clone(), costume_dims.clone(), sound_queue.clone())
        text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("{tag.label}: {value}".to_string())

# List watcher's counterpart -- unlike a variable/reporter watcher's
# single-line "<label>: <value>", real Scratch's list box shows the
# label on its own line, above the (possibly multi-line, per
# `list_watcher_display_string`'s own join rule) contents.
pub def sync_list_watchers(Query<(ListWatcherTag&, mut Text2d&, mut Visibility&)> query, Res<Lists> lists):
    for tag, text2d, visibility in query:
        let contents = list_watcher_display_string(lists.clone(), tag.list_id.clone())
        # `data_showlist`/`data_hidelist`'s own real effect -- same "write
        # the real component every tick" pattern `sync_variable_watchers`
        # already uses.
        visibility.pointee = if lists.watcher_shown(tag.list_id.clone()): Visibility.Visible else: Visibility.Hidden
        text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("{tag.label}\n{contents}".to_string())

# Keeps the ask box's real `Visibility`/`Text2d` in sync with `Vars.
# is_asking()`/`ask_buffer` every tick -- same "write the real component
# every tick" pattern `sync_visibility_and_size_from_looks_states`/`sync_
# variable_watchers` already established. A trailing `|` on the typed-so-
# far line is a plain fixed cursor glyph, not a real blinking caret (no
# animation/timer wired to it) -- a real, deliberately simple stand-in,
# not an attempt at a polished text-input widget.
pub def sync_ask_ui(Query<(AskBoxText&, mut Text2d&, mut Visibility&)> query, Res<Vars> vars):
    for tag, text2d, visibility in query:
        if vars.is_asking():
            visibility.pointee = Visibility.Visible
            var mut question = ""
            if let ask = vars.front_ask():
                question = ask.question
            text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("{question}\n{vars.ask_buffer}|".to_string())
        else:
            visibility.pointee = Visibility.Hidden
            text2d.pointee = Text2d.new("")

# Drains real keyboard character input into `Vars.ask_buffer`, submitting
# on Enter -- `sensing_askandwait`'s own text-entry half (the pausing/
# resuming half lives in `step_thread`'s `WaitingForAnswer` arm,
# `interpreter.br`). Always reads every `KeyboardInput` event every tick,
# even while nothing is asking (`Events<T>` auto-expires unread events
# after two frames regardless, so *not* draining while idle would risk a
# stray keystroke leaking into the very next ask that starts) -- acting on
# one only if `vars.is_asking()`.
#
# `event.state == ButtonState.Pressed` only -- skips the matching Release
# event every real keypress also generates (this project's own established
# edge-triggered-input convention, same as `key_option_just_pressed`'s own
# `.just_pressed()` calls elsewhere). Checked by physical `key_code`
# (`KeyCode.Enter`/`KeyCode.Backspace`), not `event.logical_key`'s own
# `Key` enum -- simpler and reuses an already-proven type this project's
# own `KeyboardState`/`check_key_press_hats` already compare `KeyCode`
# values against everywhere. Printable characters come from `event.text`
# (`Option<SmolStr>`, real Bevy's own "already resolved to real input
# text, correct across keyboard layouts" field) rather than pattern-
# matching `Key::Character` out of `logical_key` by hand.
pub def handle_ask_text_input(mut MessageReader<KeyboardInput> events, mut ResMut<Vars> vars):
    for event in events.read():
        if event.state == ButtonState.Pressed and vars.is_asking():
            if event.key_code == KeyCode.Enter:
                if let ask = vars.front_ask():
                    # Computed into a local first, not `vars.set_answer(
                    # vars.ask_buffer.clone())` inline -- the same
                    # overlapping-mutable/immutable-borrow shape `SetVar`'s
                    # own doc comment in `interpreter.br` already
                    # documents for `vars.set(id, eval(...))`, a real
                    # `cargo build` E0502 confirmed here too.
                    let submitted = vars.ask_buffer.clone()
                    vars.set_answer(submitted)
                    vars.last_answered_id = ask.ask_id
                    mut remaining = vars.ask_queue.clone()
                    remaining.remove(0)
                    vars.ask_queue = remaining
                    vars.ask_buffer = ""
            elif event.key_code == KeyCode.Backspace:
                # NOT `buf[0..end]` -- confirmed with a real `cargo build`
                # that string slicing with a *computed* (non-literal)
                # upper bound transpiles as if slicing a `Vec` (a real
                # `.to_vec()` call, E0599 against `str`), regardless of an
                # intermediate `let end = ...` binding. Popping the last
                # character off `.chars()` (already proven elsewhere in
                # this codebase, `book.md`'s own `s.chars()`/`arr.pop()`
                # methods) and rebuilding sidesteps it entirely -- a
                # no-op, safely, if the buffer's already empty (`Vec::pop`
                # on an empty vec is itself a safe no-op).
                mut chars = vars.ask_buffer.chars()
                chars.pop()
                var mut rebuilt = ""
                for c in chars:
                    rebuilt = "{rebuilt}{c}"
                vars.ask_buffer = rebuilt
            else:
                if let typed = event.text:
                    vars.ask_buffer = "{vars.ask_buffer}{typed}"

# Maps one Scratch KEY_OPTION string to whether that physical key was
# *just* pressed this tick -- `event_whenkeypressed`'s own edge-triggered
# semantics, checked directly against real Bevy input (not through
# `KeyboardState`, which is level-triggered -- see its own doc comment).
# Same tracked-key set as `sync_keyboard_state` -- space + 4 arrows + every
# letter/digit, confirmed against real `scratch-blocks` source
# (`sensing_keyoptions`'s `field_dropdown` options, `blocks/sensing.ts`)
# to be the COMPLETE set of individually nameable KEY_OPTION values (no
# punctuation option exists in the real dropdown at all -- verified, not
# assumed). `"any"` reads Bevy's own `get_just_pressed()` iterator
# directly instead of OR-ing the 41 named checks above -- true parity with
# real Scratch's own "any key at all" semantics (covers every physical key
# Bevy reports, not just the ones this project can name), same reasoning
# `KeyboardState.any_key_pressed`'s own doc comment gives for the
# level-triggered reporter side.
# Takes a plain `ButtonInput<KeyCode>` (not `Res<...>`) -- `keyboard.clone()`
# at the one call site (`check_key_press_hats`, needed to call this once per
# hat in a loop without moving `keyboard` out on the first iteration)
# resolves to cloning the *inner* `ButtonInput` through `Res`'s `Deref`, not
# `Res` itself (confirmed with a real `cargo build` before this was
# written), so this parameter type is what the call site actually produces.
def bool key_option_just_pressed(ButtonInput<KeyCode> keyboard, string key_option):
    if key_option == "space":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Space)
    elif key_option == "up arrow":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.ArrowUp)
    elif key_option == "down arrow":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.ArrowDown)
    elif key_option == "left arrow":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.ArrowLeft)
    elif key_option == "right arrow":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.ArrowRight)
    elif key_option == "a":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyA)
    elif key_option == "b":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyB)
    elif key_option == "c":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyC)
    elif key_option == "d":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyD)
    elif key_option == "e":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyE)
    elif key_option == "f":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyF)
    elif key_option == "g":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyG)
    elif key_option == "h":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyH)
    elif key_option == "i":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyI)
    elif key_option == "j":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyJ)
    elif key_option == "k":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyK)
    elif key_option == "l":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyL)
    elif key_option == "m":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyM)
    elif key_option == "n":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyN)
    elif key_option == "o":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyO)
    elif key_option == "p":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyP)
    elif key_option == "q":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyQ)
    elif key_option == "r":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyR)
    elif key_option == "s":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyS)
    elif key_option == "t":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyT)
    elif key_option == "u":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyU)
    elif key_option == "v":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyV)
    elif key_option == "w":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyW)
    elif key_option == "x":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyX)
    elif key_option == "y":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyY)
    elif key_option == "z":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.KeyZ)
    elif key_option == "0":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit0)
    elif key_option == "1":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit1)
    elif key_option == "2":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit2)
    elif key_option == "3":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit3)
    elif key_option == "4":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit4)
    elif key_option == "5":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit5)
    elif key_option == "6":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit6)
    elif key_option == "7":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit7)
    elif key_option == "8":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit8)
    elif key_option == "9":
        keyboard.just_pressed(KeyCode.Digit9)
    elif key_option == "any":
        keyboard.get_just_pressed().len() > 0
    else:
        false

# Populates `KeyboardState.pressed_keys` from real Bevy input each tick --
# the level-triggered snapshot `sensing_keypressed` reads inside `eval`.
# Tracks space + the four arrow keys + every letter/digit (real Scratch's
# KEY_OPTION dropdown also offers some punctuation keys this project still
# doesn't track -- a narrower, documented gap than the old "letters/digits
# missing entirely" one; see README.md's roadmap). Stored key strings are
# lowercase single letters/plain digit strings, matching real scratch-vm's
# own KEY_OPTION dropdown values verified against source (`key_option_
# just_pressed`'s own doc comment).
pub def sync_keyboard_state(mut ResMut<KeyboardState> state, Res<ButtonInput<KeyCode>> keyboard):
    mut pressed = []
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Space):
        pressed.push("space")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.ArrowUp):
        pressed.push("up arrow")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.ArrowDown):
        pressed.push("down arrow")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.ArrowLeft):
        pressed.push("left arrow")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.ArrowRight):
        pressed.push("right arrow")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyA):
        pressed.push("a")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyB):
        pressed.push("b")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyC):
        pressed.push("c")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyD):
        pressed.push("d")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyE):
        pressed.push("e")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyF):
        pressed.push("f")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyG):
        pressed.push("g")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyH):
        pressed.push("h")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyI):
        pressed.push("i")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyJ):
        pressed.push("j")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyK):
        pressed.push("k")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyL):
        pressed.push("l")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyM):
        pressed.push("m")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyN):
        pressed.push("n")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyO):
        pressed.push("o")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyP):
        pressed.push("p")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyQ):
        pressed.push("q")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyR):
        pressed.push("r")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyS):
        pressed.push("s")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyT):
        pressed.push("t")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyU):
        pressed.push("u")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyV):
        pressed.push("v")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyW):
        pressed.push("w")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyX):
        pressed.push("x")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyY):
        pressed.push("y")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.KeyZ):
        pressed.push("z")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit0):
        pressed.push("0")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit1):
        pressed.push("1")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit2):
        pressed.push("2")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit3):
        pressed.push("3")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit4):
        pressed.push("4")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit5):
        pressed.push("5")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit6):
        pressed.push("6")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit7):
        pressed.push("7")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit8):
        pressed.push("8")
    if keyboard.pressed(KeyCode.Digit9):
        pressed.push("9")
    state.pressed_keys = pressed
    state.any_key_pressed = keyboard.get_pressed().len() > 0

# Populates `MouseState` from real Bevy input each tick -- see
# `MouseState`'s own doc comment for the coordinate-conversion assumption
# (an unscaled default `Camera2d`, matching what `spawn_camera` actually
# sets up). `Single<Window&>` (no `With<PrimaryWindow>` filter) is enough
# since this project never spawns more than one window; a headless test
# with no `WindowPlugin` at all simply has zero matching entities, which
# `Single` treats as "skip this system for this tick" rather than a panic
# (unlike a missing `Res<T>`), so this is safe to run unconditionally.
pub def sync_mouse_state(mut ResMut<MouseState> state, Single<Window&> window, Res<ButtonInput<MouseButton>> mouse_buttons):
    state.down = mouse_buttons.pressed(MouseButton.Left)
    if let pos = window.cursor_position():
        state.x = pos.x - (window.width() / 2.0)
        state.y = (window.height() / 2.0) - pos.y

# `time.delta_secs()` -- same accessor `breakout-boring`'s own
# `apply_velocity` already uses. Real Scratch's timer resets to 0 whenever
# the green flag is clicked, which is exactly `ScratchTimer`'s own Startup
# seeding in `setup_scratch_project` -- there's no separate "reset" event
# to handle here since this project only ever runs one project load per
# `App` lifetime (`sensing_resettimer` -- a *mid-run* reset -- isn't
# implemented yet, a real gap, see README.md's roadmap).
pub def sync_scratch_timer(mut ResMut<ScratchTimer> timer, Res<Time> time, mut ResMut<SoundQueue> sound_queue):
    # `sensing_resettimer`'s own deferred request (`SoundQueue.reset_
    # timer_requested`, see its own doc comment for why it's not a direct
    # write from `exec_simple`) -- checked here, the one place that
    # already has real `mut ResMut<ScratchTimer>` access. Resets straight
    # to `0.0` and skips adding this tick's own `delta_secs()` on top --
    # a deliberate simplification (real Scratch's own reset also lands on
    # a *slightly* nonzero value by the time anything next reads it,
    # continuous wall-clock time having no tick boundaries to land
    # exactly on) rather than a materially different behavior.
    if sound_queue.reset_timer_requested:
        timer.elapsed = 0.0
        sound_queue.reset_timer_requested = false
    else:
        timer.elapsed = timer.elapsed + time.delta_secs()

# Checked directly against real Bevy input (via `key_option_just_pressed`),
# not through `KeyboardState` -- an edge-triggered check, run once per tick,
# queues each matching hat's script exactly once on the tick its key
# transitions to pressed (never re-fires while the key stays held, matching
# real Scratch's own `event_whenkeypressed` semantics).
#
# Fanned out to every live instance of each hat's own origin
# (`instances_of`, `runtime.br`), not just the origin -- real Scratch fires
# `when key pressed` on every currently-live clone of the sprite that owns
# it too, not only the original.
#
# Skipped entirely while `Vars.is_asking()` -- typing an answer into
# `sensing_askandwait`'s own on-screen box would otherwise also fire
# "when space key pressed" every time the answer happens to contain a
# space (the only tracked key realistically typed into free text), a real
# false-positive real Scratch's own ask box avoids by capturing keyboard
# focus while it's open.
pub def check_key_press_hats(Res<ButtonInput<KeyCode>> keyboard, Res<KeyPressHats> key_press_hats, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, Res<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    if !vars.is_asking():
        for hat in key_press_hats.hats.clone():
            # `keyboard.clone()` (an inherent method `Res<T>` provides
            # specifically for this -- deliberately not the `Clone`
            # trait, so it doesn't collide with cloning the *inner*
            # resource through `Res`'s `Deref`) -- needed because passing
            # `keyboard` itself would move it out on the loop's first
            # iteration, and every iteration needs it again.
            if key_option_just_pressed(keyboard.clone(), hat.key_option):
                for instance_id in instances_of(sound_queue.instance_types.clone(), hat.script.target_id.clone()):
                    let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = instance_id, body = hat.script.body.clone())
                    pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))

# `event_whenthisspriteclicked` -- edge-triggered against real Bevy input
# (`ButtonInput<MouseButton>.just_pressed`, the same primitive `check_key_
# press_hats` uses for keys) *and* an AABB hit test against the hat's own
# owning target (`touching_point`, `runtime.br` -- the exact same bounding-
# box math `sensing_touchingobject`'s mouse-touching case already uses).
# A hidden target is never clickable, matching real Scratch and the same
# visibility gate `Touching`'s own sprite-to-sprite case already applies.
# `mouse` (not raw cursor position) since `sync_mouse_state` (chained
# before this system) already converts real window pixel coordinates into
# this project's stage-centered, y-up space -- no reason to redo that
# conversion here.
#
# Also handles `event_whenstageclicked` now -- a genuinely separate
# opcode real Scratch's own editor saves for this exact block dragged
# onto the Stage (confirmed against real source, `build_click_hats`'s own
# doc comment in `linker.br`), which used to silently never fire at all.
# The Stage gets its own special-cased branch below, not the AABB/
# `TargetCostumeDims` path every sprite hat uses -- the Stage always
# fills the fixed 480x360 stage rect regardless of its own backdrop's
# real decoded pixel size, and has no `looks_hide`/`looks_show` concept
# of its own to gate on.
#
# Unlike `check_key_press_hats`/`check_greater_than_hats`, fanning out to
# live clones isn't just "spawn one more thread per instance" -- the AABB
# hit test itself has to run *per instance*, since each clone can be
# positioned differently and clicked independently, not tested once against
# the origin's own position. `costume_dims.get` still keys off the *origin*
# name, not the instance id -- costume dimensions are Type-level, shared by
# every clone of a sprite (see `SoundQueue.instance_types`' own doc
# comment), never per-instance.
pub def check_click_hats(Res<ButtonInput<MouseButton>> mouse_buttons, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<ClickHats> click_hats, Res<Positions> positions, Res<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    if mouse_buttons.just_pressed(MouseButton.Left):
        for hat in click_hats.hats.clone():
            let origin_name = hat.script.target_id.clone()
            if origin_name == "Stage":
                # `event_whenstageclicked` -- the Stage always fills the
                # whole fixed 480x360 stage rect regardless of its own
                # current backdrop's real decoded pixel size (unlike a
                # sprite costume, whose native size determines its own
                # footprint) -- a plain fixed-bounds check, not the AABB-
                # via-`TargetCostumeDims` machinery every sprite click hat
                # below needs. No visibility gate either -- the Stage has
                # no `looks_hide`/`looks_show` concept of its own.
                if mouse.x >= -240.0 and mouse.x <= 240.0 and mouse.y >= -180.0 and mouse.y <= 180.0:
                    let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = "Stage", body = hat.script.body.clone())
                    pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))
            else:
                for instance_id in instances_of(sound_queue.instance_types.clone(), origin_name.clone()):
                    let s = looks.get(instance_id.clone())
                    if s.visible:
                        let pos = positions.get(instance_id.clone())
                        let dim = costume_dims.get(origin_name.clone(), s.costume_index)
                        let scale = s.size / 100.0
                        let half_w = (dim.width * scale) / 2.0
                        let half_h = (dim.height * scale) / 2.0
                        if touching_point(pos.x, pos.y, half_w, half_h, mouse.x, mouse.y):
                            let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = instance_id, body = hat.script.body.clone())
                            pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))

# Real click-and-drag mouse interaction, unlocked by `sensing_
# setdragmode` (`SoundQueue.draggable`, a real per-instance flag a script
# can toggle -- previously a documented no-op, since no interaction
# system existed yet to read it). Picks the TOPMOST (highest `LayerOrders`
# rank) visible, draggable instance whose AABB contains the mouse on the
# tick the left button is first pressed -- same `touching_point` AABB math
# `check_click_hats` already uses for its own mouse-hit test, just fanned
# out across *every* live instance instead of only the ones with a click
# hat, and z-sorted (real Scratch always picks the front-most sprite under
# the cursor when several overlap, confirmed against real scratch-gui
# source's own `onStartDrag`/`renderer.pick`).
#
# `offset_x`/`offset_y` (`DragState`'s own doc comment) are captured once
# at pick time and re-applied every tick the button stays held, so the
# sprite stays glued to wherever it was actually grabbed rather than
# snapping its center to the cursor. Re-checks `get_draggable` every tick
# (not just at pick time) -- if a script calls `set drag mode to [not
# draggable]` while a sprite is being dragged, or the dragged clone gets
# deleted (`remove_draggable` on despawn falls back to the same `false`
# default `get_draggable` already returns for a never-draggable target),
# the drag stops cleanly instead of continuing to move a sprite that
# shouldn't be draggable anymore.
pub def handle_sprite_dragging(Res<ButtonInput<MouseButton>> mouse_buttons, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, Res<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<LayerOrders> layer_orders, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, mut ResMut<DragState> drag_state):
    if mouse_buttons.just_pressed(MouseButton.Left):
        let {string=string} instance_types = sound_queue.instance_types.clone()
        let {string=bool} draggable_now = sound_queue.draggable.clone()
        var mut best_id = ""
        var best_order = 0
        var found = false
        for id, origin_name in instance_types:
            let is_draggable = draggable_now[id.clone()] else false
            if is_draggable:
                let s = looks.get(id.clone())
                if s.visible:
                    let pos = positions.get(id.clone())
                    let dim = costume_dims.get(origin_name.clone(), s.costume_index)
                    let scale = s.size / 100.0
                    let half_w = (dim.width * scale) / 2.0
                    let half_h = (dim.height * scale) / 2.0
                    if touching_point(pos.x, pos.y, half_w, half_h, mouse.x, mouse.y):
                        let order = layer_orders.get(id.clone())
                        if !found or order > best_order:
                            best_id = id
                            best_order = order
                            found = true
        if found:
            let pos = positions.get(best_id.clone())
            drag_state.dragging_id = best_id
            drag_state.offset_x = pos.x - mouse.x
            drag_state.offset_y = pos.y - mouse.y
    if drag_state.dragging_id != "":
        if mouse_buttons.pressed(MouseButton.Left) and sound_queue.get_draggable(drag_state.dragging_id.clone()):
            let current = positions.get(drag_state.dragging_id.clone())
            positions.set(drag_state.dragging_id.clone(), Position(x = mouse.x + drag_state.offset_x, y = mouse.y + drag_state.offset_y, direction = current.direction))
        else:
            drag_state.dragging_id = ""

# Checked by actually evaluating each hat's VALUE expression against the
# full runtime state (unlike `check_key_press_hats`, there's no cheap
# direct-Bevy-resource check available here -- the threshold can be any
# expression, not just a literal). Both "TIMER" and "LOUDNESS" are
# supported -- LOUDNESS via `current_loudness()`'s own real `-1` sentinel
# (see that function's own doc comment), correct for the no-microphone
# condition this project is always in, not a guess. Any other menu value
# evaluates `false` rather than crashing.
# Rebuilds the whole `hats` vec (never an indexed/keyed assignment into an
# existing element) to update each hat's own `was_true` -- the same
# whole-collection-replace pattern `PendingThreads`/`SoundQueue` already
# use for draining, and deliberately avoids probing whether a `[T]`-index
# assignment through a `var`-parameter hits the same class of transpiler
# bug already found and worked around for dict fields (see
# `SpeechBubbles.set`'s own doc comment) -- untested for a plain list
# index, not worth risking here.
#
# The condition itself doesn't depend on which instance is running it (only
# "TIMER" is supported, a target-agnostic reporter) -- so `was_true`
# tracking stays once per *template*, but on a true transition, one thread
# is started per currently-live instance of that template's origin
# (`instances_of`, `runtime.br`), not just the origin, matching real
# Scratch's own "every live clone gets its own copy of every hat" semantics.
pub def check_greater_than_hats(mut ResMut<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<Lists> lists, Res<ProcTable> procs, Res<Broadcasts> broadcasts, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, Res<KeyboardState> keyboard, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<ScratchTimer> timer, mut ResMut<Rng> rng, mut ResMut<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumes> target_costumes, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<GreaterThanHats> greater_than_hats, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    let hats = greater_than_hats.hats.clone()
    mut new_hats = []
    for hat in hats:
        let is_true = if hat.menu == "TIMER":
            let top_frame = Frame(params = {=}, target_id = hat.script.target_id.clone())
            let threshold = eval(hat.value_expr.clone(), vars.pointee, top_frame, procs.clone(), lists.pointee, broadcasts.clone(), positions.pointee, keyboard.clone(), mouse.clone(), timer.clone(), rng.pointee, looks.pointee, target_costumes.clone(), costume_dims.clone(), sound_queue.clone()).to_num()
            ScratchNumber.from_f32(timer.elapsed).gt_owned(threshold)
        elif hat.menu == "LOUDNESS":
            # `current_loudness()`'s own doc comment (`runtime.br`) --
            # always `-1` today (no microphone subsystem), so this is
            # real, correct behavior for the condition this project is
            # always in, not a guess: matches whatever `threshold` a
            # project actually uses, same as real Scratch would.
            let top_frame = Frame(params = {=}, target_id = hat.script.target_id.clone())
            let threshold = eval(hat.value_expr.clone(), vars.pointee, top_frame, procs.clone(), lists.pointee, broadcasts.clone(), positions.pointee, keyboard.clone(), mouse.clone(), timer.clone(), rng.pointee, looks.pointee, target_costumes.clone(), costume_dims.clone(), sound_queue.clone()).to_num()
            ScratchNumber.from_f32(current_loudness()).gt_owned(threshold)
        else:
            false
        if is_true and !hat.was_true:
            for instance_id in instances_of(sound_queue.instance_types.clone(), hat.script.target_id.clone()):
                let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = instance_id, body = hat.script.body.clone())
                pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))
        new_hats.push(GreaterThanHat(menu = hat.menu, value_expr = hat.value_expr, was_true = is_true, script = hat.script))
    greater_than_hats.hats = new_hats

# `event_whentouchingobject` -- unlike `check_greater_than_hats`' own
# single template-level `was_true` (its TIMER condition is target-
# agnostic), touching-status genuinely depends on *which instance* is
# asking, so this tracks one `was_true` per live instance
# (`TouchingObjectHat.was_true_by_instance`), fanned out the same
# `instances_of` way every other hat-template system already does. Each
# instance's own transition map entry is rebuilt fresh into a new
# `{=}`-bound local every tick (never index-assigned in place into the
# old map) -- the same nested-mutable-dict transpiler workaround the
# sprite-local-variables round already established for this exact shape.
# `hat.touching_block` is `sensing_touchingobject`'s own already-linked
# `Block(kind = BlockKind.Touching(...))` -- `eval`-ing it per instance
# (via a per-instance `Frame`) reuses `Touching`'s own AABB/mouse/edge
# dispatch wholesale, same as `event_whenthisspriteclicked` already does
# for its own click-AABB check.
pub def check_touching_object_hats(mut ResMut<Vars> vars, mut ResMut<Lists> lists, Res<ProcTable> procs, Res<Broadcasts> broadcasts, mut ResMut<Positions> positions, Res<KeyboardState> keyboard, Res<MouseState> mouse, Res<ScratchTimer> timer, mut ResMut<Rng> rng, mut ResMut<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumes> target_costumes, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<TouchingObjectHats> touching_hats, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    let hats = touching_hats.hats.clone()
    mut new_hats = []
    for hat in hats:
        mut new_was_true = {=}
        let {string=bool} was_true_by_instance = hat.was_true_by_instance.clone()
        for instance_id in instances_of(sound_queue.instance_types.clone(), hat.script.target_id.clone()):
            let top_frame = Frame(params = {=}, target_id = instance_id.clone())
            let is_touching = eval(hat.touching_block.clone(), vars.pointee, top_frame, procs.clone(), lists.pointee, broadcasts.clone(), positions.pointee, keyboard.clone(), mouse.clone(), timer.clone(), rng.pointee, looks.pointee, target_costumes.clone(), costume_dims.clone(), sound_queue.clone()).truthy()
            let was_true = was_true_by_instance[instance_id.clone()] else false
            if is_touching and !was_true:
                let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = instance_id.clone(), body = hat.script.body.clone())
                pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))
            new_was_true[instance_id] = is_touching
        new_hats.push(TouchingObjectHat(touching_block = hat.touching_block, was_true_by_instance = new_was_true, script = hat.script))
    touching_hats.hats = new_hats

# `event_whenbackdropswitchesto` -- edge-detected against the Stage's own
# current backdrop name, not re-checked as a level condition the way
# `check_greater_than_hats` is (see `BackdropSwitchHats.last_backdrop_
# name`'s own doc comment for why this project needs to detect the switch
# this way at all, unlike real scratch-vm's own direct `startHats` call
# from inside `switchBackdrop`). Fans out to every live clone of a hat's
# own origin sprite, same `instances_of` pattern `check_greater_than_
# hats`/`check_key_press_hats` already use -- a backdrop switch is
# project-wide, so any clone with a matching hat should fire too, not
# just the original.
pub def check_backdrop_switch_hats(mut ResMut<BackdropSwitchHats> backdrop_hats, Res<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumes> target_costumes, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue, mut ResMut<PendingThreads> pending):
    let idx = looks.get("Stage").costume_index
    let names = target_costumes.get("Stage")
    let current_name = if idx >= 0 and idx < names.length: names[idx] else: ""
    if current_name != backdrop_hats.last_backdrop_name:
        backdrop_hats.last_backdrop_name = current_name.clone()
        # `.clone()`'d into a local first, not iterated straight off the
        # `ResMut`-wrapped field -- same "can't move out of a `ResMut`
        # dereference" shape `check_greater_than_hats`' own `let hats =
        # greater_than_hats.hats.clone()` already works around.
        let hats = backdrop_hats.hats.clone()
        for hat in hats:
            if hat.backdrop_name == current_name:
                for instance_id in instances_of(sound_queue.instance_types.clone(), hat.script.target_id.clone()):
                    let script = ReceiverScript(target_id = instance_id, body = hat.script.body.clone())
                    pending.queue.push(spawn_thread(script))

# Copies each target's `Positions` entry onto its `ScratchSprite` entity's
# `Transform` once per tick, *after* `run_pending_threads` (see `build_app`'s
# `.chain()`) -- Motion blocks mutate the plain `Positions` resource, not a
# live `Transform` directly, keeping the interpreter itself Bevy-free (see
# `Positions`' own doc comment); this system is the one and only place that
# bridges the two.
#
# Also applies real Scratch's own three rotation styles, ported from
# `RenderedTarget._getRenderedDirectionAndScale` (`rendered-target.js`),
# not guessed:
#
# - "all around": the sprite's `Transform.rotation` tracks `direction`
#   directly. Real Scratch's own rendering convention treats `direction =
#   90` (facing right) as the costume's own *unrotated* pose (confirmed by
#   `_getRenderedDirectionAndScale` forcing `finalDirection = 90` for the
#   other two styles below, i.e. "no visual rotation" -- 90 IS the zero
#   point), so the visual rotation *clockwise* from that pose is `direction
#   - 90` degrees. Bevy's `Quat::from_rotation_z` is counter-clockwise-
#   positive (standard math convention, Z pointing out of the screen), so
#   this negates that: `(90 - direction)` degrees, converted to radians.
#   Sanity-checked by hand: direction=90 (right) -> 0 rotation (matches the
#   zero point); direction=0 (up) -> +90 degrees CCW from "facing right" ->
#   faces up, correct; direction=180 (down) -> -90 degrees (CW) -> faces
#   down, correct.
# - "left-right": no rotation matrix at all (forced to the same "facing
#   right" zero point as "don't rotate" below) -- instead, `Sprite.flip_x`
#   mirrors the costume horizontally whenever `direction < 0` (real
#   Scratch's own condition, `(-180, 180]` range, exactly matching this
#   project's own `normalize_direction`'s range).
# - "don't rotate": no rotation, no flip, ever -- the sprite always renders
#   in its costume's own native orientation regardless of `direction`.
pub def sync_transforms_from_positions(Query<(mut Transform&, mut Sprite&, ScratchSprite&)> query, Res<Positions> positions, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue):
    for transform, sprite, scratch_sprite in query:
        let p = positions.get(scratch_sprite.target_id.clone())
        transform.translation.x = p.x
        transform.translation.y = p.y
        let style = sound_queue.get_rotation_style(scratch_sprite.target_id.clone())
        if style == "all around":
            let radians = (90.0 - p.direction) * 0.017453292
            transform.rotation = Quat.from_rotation_z(radians)
            sprite.flip_x = false
        else:
            transform.rotation = Quat.from_rotation_z(0.0)
            if style == "left-right":
                sprite.flip_x = p.direction < 0.0
            else:
                sprite.flip_x = false

# Same shape as `sync_transforms_from_positions`, for Looks' non-Motion
# visual state instead: `looks_show`/`looks_hide` toggle a real Bevy
# `Visibility` (so a hidden sprite genuinely doesn't render, not just a
# flag this project tracks and ignores), `looks_setsizeto`/
# `looks_changesizeby` scale the same `Transform` Motion already writes
# to (real Scratch's own `size` is a percent of the costume's original
# dimensions -- `100.0` is always "no scaling", matching `LooksState`'s
# own default).
pub def sync_visibility_and_size_from_looks_states(Query<(mut Visibility&, mut Transform&, ScratchSprite&)> query, Res<LooksStates> looks):
    for visibility, transform, sprite in query:
        let s = looks.get(sprite.target_id.clone())
        visibility.pointee = if s.visible: Visibility.Visible else: Visibility.Hidden
        let scale = s.size / 100.0
        transform.scale.x = scale
        transform.scale.y = scale

# `looks_switchcostumeto`/`looks_nextcostume` only ever mutate
# `LooksState.costume_index` (a plain integer, no Bevy types involved --
# see `interpreter.br`'s own `SwitchCostumeTo`/`NextCostume` cases); this
# is the one system that turns that back into something actually visible,
# by swapping which pre-loaded `Handle<Image>` (`TargetCostumeHandles`,
# built once at Startup) the sprite's own `Sprite` component currently
# points at. `% handles.length` (not a bare index) tolerates
# `costume_index` values `wrap_costume_index` already keeps in range for
# a real project, but stays safe even if `handles` and the linked
# project's own costume count ever disagreed for some other reason --
# indexing out of bounds would panic the whole game over a rendering
# nicety, exactly the failure mode every other costume-loading path in
# this file already avoids.
pub def sync_costume_from_looks_states(Query<(mut Sprite&, TargetCostumeHandles&, ScratchSprite&)> query, Res<LooksStates> looks, Res<TargetCostumeDims> costume_dims, Res<SoundQueue> sound_queue):
    for sprite, costumes, tag in query:
        if costumes.handles.length > 0:
            let s = looks.get(tag.target_id.clone())
            let idx = wrap_costume_index(s.costume_index, costumes.handles.length)
            sprite.image = costumes.handles[idx].clone()
            # `TargetCostumeDims` already reports a bitmap costume's real
            # *logical* size (its raw decoded PNG pixel size divided by
            # its own saved `bitmapResolution` -- `build_target_costume_
            # dims`' own doc comment in `assets.br`) -- an SVG costume's
            # own resolution is always 1, so this is a real no-op there,
            # matching the native pixel size Bevy would render at anyway
            # without `custom_size` set at all. Setting it explicitly
            # here (rather than leaving it `None`, native-pixel-size) is
            # what makes a retina-authored bitmap costume finally render
            # at its *intended* on-screen size instead of twice as large.
            #
            # `TargetCostumeDims` stays keyed by *origin* name only, same
            # as `TargetCostumes` -- resolve the entity's own target id
            # (a live clone's unique id, for a cloned entity) through
            # `instance_type_of` first, same established pattern
            # `SwitchCostumeTo`/`Touching` already use, or a clone with a
            # real (non-40x40-default) costume would render at the wrong
            # size here.
            let origin = sound_queue.instance_type_of(tag.target_id.clone())
            let dim = costume_dims.get(origin, idx)
            # Workaround for a real boring transpiler gap (originally
            # filed as task_470fa44b; the fix that landed upstream,
            # commit e0247ff, only covers a Boring-*declared* struct's
            # own T? field via `struct_fields` -- `Sprite` is an
            # *external* (Bevy) type, a different code path entirely,
            # confirmed still broken by a real build; re-filed narrower
            # as task_ff23e8cb): a direct `sprite.custom_size = Vec2.new(
            # ...)` assignment doesn't auto-wrap in `Some(...)` for an
            # external struct's own `Option<T>` field. Binding an
            # explicit `Vec2?`-typed local first still triggers the
            # correct wrap.
            let Vec2? new_size = Vec2.new(dim.width, dim.height)
            sprite.custom_size = new_size

# `LooksState.ghost` (0-100, real Scratch's own range) onto `Sprite.
# color`'s real alpha channel -- the one graphic effect this project
# renders (see that field's own doc comment in `runtime.br` for why the
# other six aren't). A separate system from `sync_visibility_and_size_
# from_looks_states` even though both read `LooksStates` and touch the
# sprite's own rendering -- same "one system per concern" split as
# `sync_costume_from_looks_states` already being its own system rather
# than folded into that one.
pub def sync_ghost_effect_from_looks_states(Query<(mut Sprite&, ScratchSprite&)> query, Res<LooksStates> looks):
    for sprite, tag in query:
        let s = looks.get(tag.target_id.clone())
        let alpha = 1.0 - (s.ghost / 100.0)
        sprite.color = sprite.color.with_alpha(alpha)

# `LayerOrders`' own per-target rank onto `Transform.translation.z` --
# no conversion needed (`LayerOrders`' own doc comment: higher already
# means "draws in front" on both sides, real Scratch's own convention
# and a 2D camera's own "higher z on top" rule).
pub def sync_z_from_layer_orders(Query<(mut Transform&, ScratchSprite&)> query, Res<LayerOrders> layer_orders):
    for transform, tag in query:
        let order = layer_orders.get(tag.target_id.clone())
        transform.translation.z = order as float32

pub def App build_app(string project_path):
    mut app = App.new()
    app.add_plugins(DefaultPlugins)
    app.insert_resource(ProjectPath(path = project_path))
    app.add_systems(Startup, (spawn_camera, spawn_pen_canvas, setup_scratch_project))
    # sync_keyboard_state/sync_mouse_state/check_key_press_hats all need to
    # run *before* run_pending_threads, so a script that reads Sensing
    # input (or a key-press hat that just queued a script) sees this same
    # tick's real values -- same ordering reason sync_transforms_from_
    # positions runs *after* it (Motion writes need to land before the
    # sprite's Transform is synced for rendering).
    # Two nested `.chain()`'d groups, not one flat 21-element tuple --
    # `sensing_askandwait`'s own two new systems pushed this past Bevy's
    # `IntoScheduleConfigs` tuple-arity ceiling (20, same family of hard
    # tuple-impl limit as the 16-parameter `SystemParam` one, already
    # flagged as a "next feature needs to restructure this" risk when
    # `spawn_pending_clones`/`despawn_pending_clones` brought this chain
    # to 19). A `.chain()`'d tuple only orders *within* itself -- nesting
    # two of them inside an outer tuple and `.chain()`ing that too orders
    # group-against-group (every system in group 1 before every system in
    # group 2) while still preserving each group's own internal order,
    # which is what a single flat chain was doing before. `handle_ask_
    # text_input` sits right before `run_pending_threads` (an Enter
    # pressed this tick should unblock the waiting thread the same tick,
    # not one tick late, same reasoning `check_key_press_hats`'s own
    # placement already established); `sync_ask_ui` sits with every other
    # `sync_*` system, after `run_pending_threads` has run.
    app.add_systems(Update, (
        (sync_keyboard_state, sync_mouse_state, sync_scratch_timer, check_key_press_hats, check_greater_than_hats, check_touching_object_hats, check_click_hats, check_backdrop_switch_hats, handle_ask_text_input, run_pending_threads, handle_sprite_dragging, draw_pen_marks, draw_stamps, play_queued_sounds, spawn_pending_clones, despawn_pending_clones).chain(),
        (sync_transforms_from_positions, sync_visibility_and_size_from_looks_states, sync_costume_from_looks_states, sync_ghost_effect_from_looks_states, sync_z_from_layer_orders, sync_speech_bubbles, sync_variable_watchers, sync_reporter_watchers, sync_list_watchers, sync_ask_ui).chain(),
        # Rebuilds `SceneComposite` (`sensing_touchingcolor`'s own real
        # data source) last, once every position/costume mutation for
        # this tick has already settled -- see that resource's own doc
        # comment (`runtime.br`) for why this timing is correct, not just
        # convenient.
        compose_scene_for_touching
    ).chain())
    app

pub def run_game(string project_path):
    build_app(project_path).run()

# The real crate entry point -- see breakout-boring's own identical comment
# for why a plain top-level statement here (not a hand-written src/main.rs)
# is what makes `boring`'s auto-appended `fn main()` the real `[[bin]]`
# entry point. `assets/Fibonacci_1.sb3` (a real `.sb3`, not a hand-authored
# raw-JSON fixture) is the default demo project -- switched from
# `assets/broadcasts.json` once real costume loading landed, since a raw
# JSON fixture has no actual asset bytes to ever show off (see
# `extract_current_costume_to_temp_file`'s own doc comment) and this is the
# only fixture with any. A real project picker is
# still out of scope for this vertical slice.
def main():
    # `args()` -- a real Boring builtin (`docs/book.md`'s own "Global
    # functions" list) -- confirmed via the generated Rust that it maps onto
    # plain `std::env::args()`, argv[0]-style: `argv[0]` is this binary's own
    # invoked path (not a user argument), so the `.sb3` path -- if given -- is
    # `argv[1]`. (This project used to read `argv[0]` directly, back when
    # `args()` excluded the binary's own path; that off-by-one bug is gone
    # now that the convention changed, not reintroduced by this comment.)
    # Falls back to `assets/Fibonacci_1.sb3` (the only checked-in fixture
    # with real costume/sound assets) when none is given, same default this
    # project always had before this was a real, runtime-configurable choice
    # rather than a hardcoded literal.
    let argv = args()
    let path = if argv.length > 1: argv[1] else: "assets/Fibonacci_1.sb3"
    run_game(path)

# ECS-test convenience: resolve a Stage variable's id by name (same lookup
# `run_project_and_report` does) but read it out of an already-populated
# `Vars` instead of building a fresh one -- lets `tests/ecs_integration.rs`
# assert on runtime state reached through the real Bevy `Update` system
# (`setup_scratch_project`/`run_pending_threads`), the same way
# `tests/broadcasts.rs` asserts on state reached through a direct
# `run_script` call. Re-loading the project just to resolve the name->id
# mapping is a little wasteful but simple and correct -- this is test-only
# plumbing, not part of the runtime's own API.
pub def string stage_var_value(string path, Vars vars, string var_name):
    if let project = Sb3Project.load_json_for_boring(path):
        let var_id = find_stage_var_id(project, var_name)
        let value = vars.get(var_id, "")
        "{value}"
    else:
        ""

# Same ECS-test convenience as `stage_var_value`, but for a target's
# `Position` -- no project re-load needed here at all, unlike `Vars`/
# `Lists` (which are keyed by opaque ids resolved from a human name via the
# project), `Positions` is already keyed directly by target name.
pub def string position_value(Positions positions, string target_name, string axis):
    let p = positions.get(target_name)
    if axis == "x":
        "{p.x}"
    elif axis == "y":
        "{p.y}"
    else:
        "{p.direction}"

# ECS-test convenience, the write-side counterpart to `position_value` --
# `Position` itself isn't `pub` (it's a plain internal runtime value, never
# constructed from outside `runtime.br`/`interpreter.br`), so a test driving
# a target's position directly across ticks (rather than through a scripted
# Motion block, the way `tests/touching_object_hat.rs` needs for its own
# edge-triggered-hat check) has no other way to build one.
pub def set_position_value(var Positions positions, string target_name, float32 x, float32 y):
    let current = positions.get(target_name)
    positions.set(target_name, Position(x = x, y = y, direction = current.direction))

